{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "\"Readers of the Observer can access a unique Sudoku puzzle in the print edition of the newspaper. This puzzle has its own set of rules, which differ from the traditional Sudoku puzzle. The main difference is that the numbers contained within grey lines must add up to the figures in the corner. This variation requires a different approach to solving the puzzle.\"\n\n\nAddition to the challenge is the restriction that no number can be repeated within each shape formed by grey lines. This adds an extra layer of complexity to the puzzle, as solvers must ensure that each shape contains a unique combination of numbers. As a result, the puzzle requires a high degree of skill and strategic thinking.\n\n\nThe Observer Digital Edition will be available next week, offering readers the opportunity to see the completed puzzle and challenge themselves against others. This will provide a new way for enthusiasts to engage with the puzzle and test their skills.\n\n\nThe unique Sudoku puzzle has been designed to provide an exciting and challenging experience for readers. By combining the traditional Sudoku rules with this new variation, the puzzle offers a fresh twist on a classic game. Solvers will need to think creatively and strategically to overcome the additional challenges posed by the grey lines.\n\n\nFor those who enjoy a good puzzle, the Observer offers a unique and engaging challenge. The Sudoku puzzle is just one example of the many puzzles and games that can be found in the newspaper and online edition. Whether you're a seasoned enthusiast or just starting to explore the world of puzzles, there's something for everyone at the Observer.\"", "logprobs": [{"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.12210632115602493}, {"token": "Read", "logprob": -0.5173866152763367}, {"token": "ers", "logpr